---
// The playground: run the benchmark on the reader's own machine. This is the ONE reading page
// that may register the service worker (to enable multithreading, with one reload); it is
// deliberately not one of the paper's chapters, so those stay reload-free. The logic lives in
// src/playground/.
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import results from '../../data/results.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
// The reference run's result hash for each size it measured: the playground checks its own
// result against it when the size matches.
const reference = Object.fromEntries(
  results.parameters.sizes.map((n) => [n, results.runs.find((run) => run.n === n)?.hash]),
);
const levels = ['Level 0: naive', 'Level 1: threaded', 'Level 2: cache blocking', 'Level 3: SIMD', 'Level 4: packed B', 'Level 5: streaming'];
---
<Page title="Playground">
  <p>
    Run the benchmark on your own machine. This page runs the same Rust code as the Methods,
    compiled to WebAssembly, inside your browser, and times each level on a matrix size you
    choose. Browser timings are indicative: they depend on your hardware, your browser and what
    else the machine is doing, and will differ from the reference run on the Results page.
  </p>
  <section class="pg-experiments" aria-labelledby="pg-experiments-title">
    <h2 id="pg-experiments-title">Experiments to try</h2>
    <p>Guess the result before you press Run, then compare.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Sweep the size.</strong> Run every level at 64, 256 and 1024. At which size does
        level 2 start to pull away from level 0?</li>
      <li><strong>Take the blocking away.</strong> Open "Cache blocks and tiles" and set both block
        sizes to 1024, at n = 1024 (at the default 256, B is only 512 KiB). Level 2 then works on blocks far larger than a typical L1 cache. How much of
        its gain over level 0 is left? This separates reading memory in order from reusing a small
        block.</li>
      <li><strong>Count the threads.</strong> Run levels 1 to 5 with 1, 2, 4 and 8 threads. Where does
        each level stop gaining? Use the threaded build (the single-threaded fallback has one
        thread). Your browser does not tell the page how many physical cores you have; compare
        with the specification of your processor.</li>
      <li><strong>Pack or not.</strong> Run levels 3 and 4 at 256, 512 and 1024. Level 4 copies each
        block of the second matrix into a contiguous buffer before reading it, and level 3 reads it
        in place. At which size, if any, does the copy pay for itself on your machine?</li>
      <li><strong>Shrink the tile.</strong> Set the tile edges of level 5 to 8 instead of 128. What
        happens to its time, and why might that be?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
  <section id="playground" data-base={base} data-reference={JSON.stringify(reference)}>
    <div id="pg-banner" class="pg-banner" role="status" hidden></div>
    <form id="pg-form" class="pg-form">
      <label>Matrix size
        <select name="n">
          {[64, 128, 256, 512, 1024].map((n) => <option value={n}>{n} × {n}</option>)}
        </select>
      </label>
      <label>Repeats (the median is shown)
        <select name="repeats">
          {[1, 3, 5].map((r) => <option value={r}>{r}</option>)}
        </select>
      </label>
      <label>Threads
        <input type="number" name="threads" min="1" max="8" value="4" />
      </label>
      <fieldset>
        <legend>Levels to run</legend>
        {levels.map((name, level) => (
          <label class="pg-check"><input type="checkbox" name="levels" value={level} checked /> {name}</label>
        ))}
      </fieldset>
      <details class="pg-advanced">
        <summary>Cache blocks and tiles</summary>
        <label>Cache block k <input type="number" name="block-k" min="1" max="1024" value="32" /></label>
        <label>Cache block j <input type="number" name="block-j" min="1" max="1024" value="64" /></label>
        <label>Level 5 tile rows <input type="number" name="tile-rows" min="1" max="1024" value="128" /></label>
        <label>Level 5 tile columns <input type="number" name="tile-cols" min="1" max="1024" value="128" /></label>
        <label>Level 5 tile depth <input type="number" name="tile-depth" min="1" max="1024" value="128" /></label>
      </details>
      <div class="pg-actions">
        <button type="submit" id="pg-run">Run</button>
        <button type="button" id="pg-cancel" disabled>Cancel</button>
      </div>
    </form>
    <p id="pg-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p id="pg-build" class="pg-build"></p>
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Playground results" hidden>
      <table class="results" id="pg-table" hidden>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">Median</th><th scope="col">Throughput</th>
            <th scope="col">vs level 0</th><th scope="col">vs previous</th><th scope="col">Time</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p id="pg-verdict" class="pg-verdict" role="status" hidden></p>
  </section>
  <script>
    import '../../playground/main.js';
  </script>
</Page>
